    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Compaction localization in sandstone has been gaining recognition as a potentially important impediment to fluid flow in subsurface reservoirs and aquifers. At the same time, the phenomenon has captured the attention of experimentalists trying to understand the conditions and mechanisms that govern its occurrence in porous, granular materials. With a few notable exceptions, however, these laboratory- based efforts have neglected field evidence and strayed from representing the process as it occurs in nature.\r\n\r\nSpecifically, most experimentation has focused on the initiation of localized compaction in moderately to well-cemented sandstones as a transition from homogeneous brittle to heterogeneous ductile deformation in accordance with bifurcation theory. As such, the mean stresses attained and strains induced generally far exceed what seems reasonable based on field evidence, and the compaction features produced bear scant resemblance to real compaction bands.\r\n\r\nTo address these shortfalls, we are pursuing a program of innovative experimentation and collaboration based on our recognition of natural compaction bands in the Aztec Sandstone at the Valley of Fire, NV as discrete “anti-cracks” that initiate and propagate in response to stress and material inhomogeneities. To date, a few promising results have been obtained, notably at SUNY Stony Brook and the University of Wisconsin, where the introduction of stress-concentrating flaws has produced discrete compaction bands away from the specimen boundaries. Our own preliminary attempts to create compaction bands in Aztec specimens demonstrate that we have yet to achieve an experimental design that reasonably mimics natural material and loading conditions, although our results point in promising directions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
